em all and allow  the market to decide where the assets  will flow that competition for assets  flowing into a bitcoin etf it's going to  come down to a lot of different things  some of it is going to be around the  custody there may be institutions that  worry about a specific custody provider  or more attracted to another custody  provider there's going to be things like  fees if one charges two and a half or  three percent on the fund and somebody  else charges one you obviously get a  better financial performance from a fee  standpoint and then lastly is what i  call familiarity right what is the thing  that gives you peace of mind there are  certain brands that have filed for a  bitcoin etf that everyone knows their  household names among institutional  investors and so they probably feel more  comfortable allocating to those funds  compared to a startup or some sort of  brand new entrant to the market i can  argue and so do many others that that's  not fair or that isn't necessarily the  best investment strategy but i think  that will absolutely play into the  institutional investor's decision as to  where they put their dollars when it  comes to a bitcoin etf now i also think  that we not only are going to see brand  new funds get the approval and start the  bitcoin etf we know that there's a  number of bitcoin etfs that have been  approved in canada and other markets uh  and then on top of that we've also seen  things like the bitcoin trust or other  crypto asset trusts from bitwise etc  that are on u.s exchanges and so when  you start to look at what's already in  the market even though there are no  bitcoin etfs available in the united  states markets you could quickly see  people taking the ones in canada trying  to cross-list them into the u.s and you  could also see the current asset  managers that have traded trust in the  united states converting them to etf  structures that provide the redemptions  and other things that etfs provide and  so ultimatel